<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch02_P03_Definitions_in_action</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>(a) Use the definitions above to decide whether each statement is true or false.</p>
<ol>
<li><p>{@stm1@}</p>
<div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>{@stm2@}</p>
<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
<li><p>{@stm3@}</p>
<div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div></li>
<li><p>{@stm4@}</p>
<div>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div></li>
</ol>
<p>(b) Give an example of an integer that is divisible by {@pp@} and by {@qq@}, but not by {@pp*qq@}.</p>
<div>[[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</div>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Each definition says that a number can be written in a certain form <em>for some integer</em>: there must be at least one integer that makes the equation true.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>An integer \(n\) is even if \(n = 2k\) for some integer \(k\), and odd if \(n = 2k + 1\) for some integer \(k\).</p></li>
<li><p>An integer \(a\) divides an integer \(b\) if \(b = a \times q\) for some integer \(q\).</p></li>
<li><p>A real number is rational if it can be written as \(\dfrac{p}{q}\), where \(p\) and \(q\) are integers and \(q \ne 0\).</p></li>
</ul>
<p>(a)</p>
<ol>
<li><p>{@stm1@} {@labtf(tr1)@} {@expl1@}</p></li>
<li><p>{@stm2@} {@labtf(tr2)@} {@expl2@}</p></li>
<li><p>{@stm3@} {@labtf(tr3)@} {@expl3@}</p></li>
<li><p>{@stm4@} {@labtf(tr4)@} {@expl4@}</p></li>
</ol>
<p>(b) {@tl@} is divisible by {@pp@} and by {@qq@}, and it is not divisible by {@pp*qq@}, because {@pp@} and {@qq@} share a factor. Divisible by both does mean divisible by the product when the two numbers have no common factor other than 1, such as 2 and 3: later proofs use exactly that fact.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
